# ( g dg/dpsi )( psi ) for examples/mhd-rectangle.toml -- the toroidal-field
# half of a tabulated-profile MHD equilibrium, against psi in webers per radian.
#
# g( psi ) = r B_toroidal is the toroidal field function, and what the
# Grad-Shafranov source contains is the PRODUCT g dg/dpsi, which is what EQDSK
# calls FF'. meq takes it exactly as tabulated:
#
#     F( r, z, psi ) = mu0 r^2 ( dp/dpsi )( psi ) + ( g dg/dpsi )( psi )
#
# -- NO mu0 on this term, and NO sign applied to either. The pressure half is
# examples/mhd-pprime.dat, and it carries the units note that goes with mu0.
#
# THE FORMAT is examples/mhd-pprime.dat's and is documented there and in
# examples/rotating-density.dat: one knot per line, `psi f( psi ) f'( psi )`,
# whitespace separated, '#' comments, a BLANK LINE TERMINATING THE TABLE -- so
# there is no blank line anywhere above the numbers below.
#
# UNITS. g dg/dpsi is in T^2 m^2 per Wb/rad, which is meq's own unit for it, so
# no scale is needed and GGPrimeScale is left at its default of 1.0 in the
# configuration. That is the contrast with examples/mhd-pprime.dat, which is
# tabulated in MPa per Wb/rad and does need one: the scale exists so that a
# table can stay in the units it was produced in, not because meq requires one.
# psi is in Wb/rad and is never scaled, so the slope column is in T^2 m^2 per
# ( Wb/rad )^2.
#
# THIS PROFILE IS WHAT KEEPS THE RUN OFF THE TRIVIAL BRANCH, and it is the only
# thing that does. The pressure gradient beside it goes as psi^2 and so vanishes
# at psi = 0, which is the boundary; if this table vanished there too then
# F( r, z, 0 ) would be identically zero, psi = 0 would solve the homogeneous
# Dirichlet problem, and Newton -- which starts from the Dirichlet datum --
# would land on it and stop in zero iterations having reported success. That is
# the failure CLAUDE.md records under Traps for every source of GS-2 sections
# 4.2 to 4.5. Here ( g dg/dpsi )( 0 ) = 1.35, so F( r, z, 0 ) = 1.35 T^2 m^2 per
# Wb/rad everywhere, the trivial solution is not a solution, and no initial guess
# is needed.
#
# IT IS AFFINE ON PURPOSE, WHICH IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE DIVISION OF LABOUR.
#
#     g dg/dpsi = 4.5 ( 0.3 - psi )  T^2 m^2 per Wb/rad
#
# is linear, so it contributes a CONSTANT -4.5 to dF/dpsi and none of the
# Jacobian's psi-dependence: every bit of the nonlinearity this example
# demonstrates comes from the quadratic dp/dpsi next door. Keeping the two
# separable is what lets the header of examples/mhd-rectangle.toml say which
# term does what. The Hermite cubic reproduces a linear function exactly, so the
# interpolant below IS 4.5( 0.3 - psi ).
#
# The slope is negative -- g dg/dpsi falls from 1.35 at the edge to 0.76 on the
# magnetic axis, about 44% -- which is the diamagnetic direction, the plasma
# pressure pushing the toroidal field down where the pressure is highest. It
# reaches zero exactly at the last knot and is positive everywhere the solution
# actually visits.
#
# THE TABLE ENDS WELL ABOVE THE SOLUTION'S RANGE. meq::SplineProfile extends by
# a CONSTANT outside its knots -- value clamped, DERIVATIVE ZERO -- so past the
# last knot this term would freeze at 0.0 and stop contributing to F at all,
# silently. psi runs over [ -1.5e-04, 0.1315 ] on the converged solution here,
# so [ -0.05, 0.30 ] is more than twice the range at the top and the clamp is
# never met. The two tables deliberately share one knot set for that reason:
# they have to cover the same interval, and a reader comparing them line by line
# should not have to interpolate in his head.
#
# psi [Wb/rad]    g dg/dpsi [T^2 m^2 / (Wb/rad)]    d/dpsi [T^2 m^2 / (Wb/rad)^2]
   -0.05                  1.575                              -4.5
    0.00                  1.350                              -4.5
    0.05                  1.125                              -4.5
    0.10                  0.900                              -4.5
    0.15                  0.675                              -4.5
    0.20                  0.450                              -4.5
    0.25                  0.225                              -4.5
    0.30                  0.000                              -4.5
